fcitx5-ngosen (1:1.7.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * On GNOME, Sen uses surrounding text when the app reports the current word correctly,
    avoiding delayed Backspace keys that corrupt text. If the app ignores deletion, it repairs
    leftover text with Backspace and uses Backspace for the rest of the session; if no deletion
    method remains, it passes keystrokes through without conversion. KDE and other Wayland
    desktops are unchanged (#443, #446).
  * Remove pauses after each deletion and commit in apps that delete through surrounding text,
    including LibreOffice and GTK4 text fields (#438).
  * Fix Sen corrupting words when apps report surrounding text later than keystrokes,
    including LibreOffice and GTK4 text fields (#441).
  * System checks warn when GNOME 50.0–50.3 runs without the forward-keys fix, which prevents
    lost Enter keys after Preedit text and lost Sen Backspace keys, and provide a command
    to enable it. Error reports include the GNOME Shell version and fix status (#440).
  * System checks no longer warn about IBus running alongside fcitx5 when it only serves other
    programs, such as IBus started by xpra; warn only when IBus competes for the current
    session's input method (#442).
  * Keep each event in saved typing logs on one line, including Enter keys and app-reported
    text containing line breaks (#445).
  * On GNOME with older fcitx5 versions (Ubuntu 26.04, Debian), typing modes selected from the
    comma menu or mode-switch key apply to all apps until logout and are no longer saved
    permanently. The next login restores the default from settings, and old saved rules
    that overrode it are ignored (#444).

 -- Nguyen Phi <nguyenphidt@gmail.com>  Fri, 30 Oct 2026 18:00:00 +0700

